  "reason": "The records provide several relevant pieces but not the complete result. R2, R3, and R18 establish the same AlCoCrFeNi2.1 FCC/B2 eutectic architecture and its dislocation-mediated mechanical behavior. R10 and R15 show that 600 °C annealing can drive decomposition and hardening in a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy, while R14 establishes thermally stable L12/gamma-prime ordering in a high-entropy alloy. Most importantly, R11 directly connects FCC/B2 interface character with stress concentration, dislocation transfer, cracking, and ductility in a closely related alloy. R6-R9, R12, and R19 add general links among modulus, ordering or planar defects, dislocation mobility, temperature, and recovery. Thus an expert could reasonably investigate intermediate-temperature aging, ordering, and interface-controlled embrittlement. However, none of the records identifies enhanced near-Ni3Al L12 ordering together with jagged FCC/B2 boundaries in AlCoCrFeNi2.1, nor predicts the specific 600 °C ductility minimum and 750 °C recovery. Connecting these elements into the non-monotonic ductile-brittle-ductile transition would still require a substantial new idea."
